<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taihang Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taihang Mountains are a range in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> running about 400 km north to south between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> plateau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. Their passes controlled the routes between plateau and plain, and in the Zhou period the uplands were home to the Di peoples. In the 20th century they sheltered Communist bases during the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains.jpg" alt="Taihang Mountains" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitsuha · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%AA%E8%A1%8C%E5%B1%B1%E9%B3%A5%E7%9E%B0_(%E6%B2%B3%E5%8D%97%E8%BC%9D%E7%B8%A3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
